  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nRadar mosaic has shown a persistent band of showers and isolated \nstorms impacting areas north of a Macomb to Pontiac line most of the \nmorning into early this afternoon. This activity has been fed by a \n30-40 kt low-level positioned from central Kansas into northern \nIllinois and should come to an end here soon as the low-level\njet continues to wane. Temperatures were reduced near/north of \nthe I-74 corridor for today due to lingering precipitation and \ncloud cover, but areas south of there are already observing \nvalues in the upper 80s to low 90s as of 1 pm.\n\nA ridge of high pressure centered over the Southern Plains will\nspread east this week, bringing a stretch of hot and mostly dry\nconditions. Temperatures this week will peak in the 90s with \nthe hottest days expected by midweek. Dewpoints in the upper 60s\nto low 70s will send heat indices over 100 degrees as early as \nMonday in west-central Illinois and then areawide Tuesday, \ncontinuing through at least Thursday. High temperatures look to \nremain just shy of records at our official climate sites, but \novernight lows in the mid 70s will likely tie or break our \nwarmest low records.\n\nA compact northern stream trough will track through the southern \nCanadian Prairies/Northern Plains this week, sparking \noccasional chances for showers and storms north of our area. The\nupper ridge will flatten out some late in the week as the \ntrough tracks north of the Great Lakes Region, allowing \nshortwave energy to intrude further south. The NBM shows low \nchances for precipitation Thursday night through the start of \nthe weekend before the ridge reamplifies later this weekend.",
